  "raw_text": "Step 1: Identify the shaded regions.\nThe shaded region consists of two triangles within the rectangle formed by twelve 1 by 1 squares.\n\nStep 2: Calculate the area of the first triangle.\nThe first triangle has a base of 2 units and a height of 4 units.\nArea = 1/2 * base * height = 1/2 * 2 * 4 = 4 square units.\n\nStep 3: Calculate the area of the second triangle.\nThe second triangle has a base of 3 units and a height of 4 units.\nArea = 1/2 * base * height = 1/2 * 3 * 4 = 6 square units.\n\nStep 4: Add the areas of both triangles to find the total shaded area.\nTotal shaded area = 4 + 6 = 10 square units.\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{10}<|im_end|>",
  "reforward_peak_bytes": 15450396160,
  "script_sha256": "3d46237d3fa2d1d194ae8a3f4996120d3c1bf0d83b3c078c151f6660db552efa",
  "source": {
    "answer": "10",
    "level": 4,
    "problem": "Twelve 1 by 1 squares form a rectangle, as shown. What is the total area of the shaded region? [asy]\nsize(4cm);defaultpen(linewidth(0.75));\n\n// Filled portions\nfill((0, 4)--(0, 0)--(2, 0)--cycle, gray(0.75));\nfill((0, 4)--(3, 4)--(3, 0)--cycle, gray(0.75));\n\n// grid\nint j;\nfor (j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {draw((j, 0)--(j, 4));}\nfor (j = 0; j < 5; ++j) {draw((0, j)--(3, j));}\n\n//diagonals\ndraw((0, 4)--(3, 0)); draw((0, 4)--(2, 0));\n[/asy]",
    "solution": "The total area of the rectangle is $3 \\times 4 =12$.\n\nThe total area of the shaded regions equals the total area of the rectangle (12) minus the area of the unshaded region.\n\nThe unshaded region is a triangle with base of length 1 and height 4; the area of this region is $\\frac{1}{2}(1)(4)=2$.\n\nTherefore, the total area of the shaded regions is $12 - 2 = \\boxed{10}$.",
    "subject": "Prealgebra",
